prompt:
You are a financial analyst revising a forecast based on a peer critique.

Original forecast: {
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "base_year": 2021,
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "forecast": [
    {
      "year": 2022,
      "revenue_cr": 7568,
      "net_income_cr": 1650,
      "eps": 8.24
    },
    {
      "year": 2023,
      "revenue_cr": 7946,
      "net_income_cr": 1733,
      "eps": 8.66
    },
    {
      "year": 2024,
      "revenue_cr": 8343,
      "net_income_cr": 1820,
      "eps": 9.09
    }
  ],
  "top_assumptions": [
    "Interest income (dominant revenue driver at ~99% of total income) recovers modestly at ~5% CAGR as HUDCO expands its loan book under PMAY-U and urban infrastructure mandates, reversing the FY2021 decline from Rs 7,483 cr to Rs 7,173 cr in interest income caused by pandemic-related disbursement slowdowns and lower borrowing costs passed through.",
    "Net profit margin stabilises around 22-23% of Total Income as credit costs normalise (FY2021 saw a write-back of Rs 73.63 cr in impairment versus a Rs 155.76 cr charge in FY2020, a one-off tailwind), deferred tax liabilities of Rs 226.64 cr moderate in subsequent years, and finance costs remain contained given the shift toward lower-cost bond issuances."
  ],
  "reasoning": "This is HUDCO, a government NBFC/HFC; Total Income (Interest Earned + Other Income) is used as the revenue line per the BANKS/NBFCs rule, giving FY2021 Total Income of Rs 7,277.73 cr versus Rs 7,571.64 cr in FY2020 \u2014 a year-on-year decline of ~3.9% driven by pandemic-related slowdown in disbursements, lower interest rates, and reduced borrowings. Only two years of P&L data are available in the report, so a multi-year CAGR cannot be precisely computed; however, the FY2021 dip is treated as anomalous (COVID-19 impact) and mean-reversion toward pre-pandemic trend is assumed. A ~5% annual growth rate is applied to Total Income reflecting HUDCO's captive government-backed pipeline (PMAY-U, Smart Cities, urban infra), partially offset by the risk that high leverage (debt securities of Rs 58,058 cr + borrowings of Rs 2,898 cr against equity of ~Rs 12,344 cr) constrains aggressive balance-sheet expansion, and that normalisation of impairment charges and elevated deferred tax will compress net profit from the FY2021 base of Rs 1,578.58 cr. EPS is computed on the stable share count implied by paid-up capital of Rs 2,001.90 cr at Rs 10 face value (~200.19 cr shares), yielding FY2021 base EPS of Rs 7.89 as confirmed in the report."
}
Critique: {
  "company": "HUDCO",
  "base_year": 2021,
  "strategy": "alt_strategy",
  "assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Interest income recovers and grows at ~5% CAGR driven by PMAY-U and urban infrastructure loan book expansion, reversing the FY2021 pandemic-driven decline.",
      "failure_scenario": "PMAY-U disbursements remain structurally sluggish beyond FY2022 due to state-level fiscal stress, delays in urban local body project approvals, and competitive pressure from cheaper bank credit, resulting in loan book stagnation and interest income growing below 2% CAGR or declining further rather than recovering to pre-pandemic trajectory.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Net profit margin stabilises at 22-23% as credit costs normalise from the one-off FY2021 impairment write-back, deferred tax liabilities moderate, and finance costs remain contained via lower-cost bond issuances.",
      "failure_scenario": "Asset quality deteriorates in HUDCO's state government and urban infrastructure loan book as overleveraged state agencies face fiscal stress post-pandemic, triggering a resumption of material impairment charges well above the FY2020 level of Rs 155.76 cr; simultaneously, rising interest rates in FY2022-23 increase finance costs on refinancing of the Rs 58,058 cr debt securities, compressing margins well below 20%.",
      "risk": "High"
    },
    {
      "assumption": "Share count remains stable at ~200.19 crore shares (implied by Rs 2,001.90 cr paid-up capital at Rs 10 face value), keeping EPS directly proportional to net income growth.",
      "failure_scenario": "The Government of India or HUDCO management executes a follow-on public offering or rights issue to shore up capital adequacy ratios given the high leverage (debt-to-equity implied at roughly 5x), materially diluting the share count and suppressing EPS growth even if net income meets forecast targets.",
      "risk": "Medium"
    }
  ],
  "confidence_score": 38,
  "overall_critique": "The forecast rests on optimistic mean-reversion logic with only two years of P&L data, making the 5% CAGR assumption for a structurally government-dependent NBFC with elevated leverage and uncertain asset quality largely speculative rather than empirically grounded. The treatment of FY2021 impairment write-backs and deferred tax as transient one-offs without stress-testing the downside adequately understates the risk that margin compression could be persistent, rendering the 22-23% net profit margin assumption the single most fragile pillar of this forecast."
}

Step 1: For each "High" risk assumption in the critique, adjust the corresponding forecast values by a conservative factor (reduce growth by 20-30% for that driver).
Step 2: For "Medium" risk assumptions, reduce growth by 10% for that driver.
Step 3: Leave "Low" risk assumptions unchanged.
Step 4: Calculate a confidence range: ± percentage based on the number of High (±15%) and Medium (±8%) risks.

RESPONSE FORMAT: Begin your response DIRECTLY with the opening brace { — no reasoning or text before the JSON.

Return ONLY valid JSON:
{
  "strategy": "<strategy>",
  "base_year": <YYYY>,
  "revised_forecast": [
    {"year": <YYYY>, "revenue_cr": <number>, "net_income_cr": <number>, "eps": <number>,
     "confidence_range_pct": <number>},
    ...
  ],
  "revisions_made": ["<description of each change>"],
  "final_confidence_score": <0-100>
}
